  excerpt: "HVAC demand swings dramatically by season, and most companies either overspend chasing summer AC leads or go quiet during the slow months. Here is a quarter-by-quarter campaign calendar built to smooth revenue across the full year.",
  content: `<h2>Why HVAC Needs a Seasonal Calendar, Not a Single Campaign</h2>
<p>Most HVAC companies run essentially the same Google Ads and Meta campaigns year-round with minor tweaks, which means they're overpaying during predictable demand spikes and leaving revenue on the table during the shoulder seasons when competitors go quiet. Search volume for "AC repair near me" can be 4-5x higher in July than in March in much of the country, and cost-per-click follows demand closely — bidding the same way in both months either wastes budget in the off-season or gets outbid during peak.</p>
<p>The fix is a quarter-by-quarter calendar that shifts budget, messaging, and channel mix to match what homeowners are actually searching for and worried about at each point in the year. Below is a framework built around the standard four-season US HVAC cycle, adjustable for regional climate differences.</p>

<h2>Q1: Maintenance Agreements and Furnace Tune-Ups</h2>
<p>January through March is the highest-opportunity, lowest-competition window of the year. Heating systems are under heavy use and starting to show strain, but most competitors have shifted focus away from proactive marketing. This is the ideal quarter to push maintenance agreements — annual service plans that typically run $150-$300 and lock in recurring revenue plus priority scheduling status with the customer.</p>
<ul>
<li>Run Google Ads around "furnace tune-up," "heating maintenance," and "why is my furnace making noise" — repair-adjacent searches that convert well into service calls.</li>
<li>Offer a discounted maintenance plan enrollment bundled with a free inspection to drive volume during the slower months.</li>
<li>Target existing customers who haven't booked a service in 12+ months with an email and SMS win-back campaign — this list typically converts at a lower cost than any paid channel.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Q2: AC Prep and Early-Bird Installs</h2>
<p>April through June is the pre-season window where smart homeowners get ahead of summer by scheduling AC inspections and, critically, where the biggest-ticket sales of the year happen: full system replacements. Homeowners considering a new AC unit who buy before peak season avoid emergency pricing and installation backlogs, and many manufacturers run early-season rebates that create a genuine urgency angle.</p>
<ul>
<li>Shift Google Ads budget toward higher-value terms like "AC replacement cost," "new air conditioner installation," and "central air unit price" — these carry lower search volume than repair terms but far higher deal value, often $5,000-$12,000+ per install.</li>
<li>Promote manufacturer rebates and 0% financing offers prominently, since financing availability is frequently the deciding factor on a replacement decision.</li>
<li>Increase budget roughly 20-30% over Q1 baseline as competition starts to build ahead of peak summer.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Q3: Peak Cooling Season and Emergency Service Ads</h2>
<p>July through September is peak demand and peak competition. This is when emergency repair campaigns need to dominate — a homeowner whose AC fails during a heatwave is calling the first company that answers and can get there same-day, and price sensitivity drops sharply in a true emergency.</p>
<ul>
<li>Run dedicated "AC repair today," "emergency AC service," and "AC not cooling" campaigns with aggressive bids and call-only ad formats — expect cost-per-click to peak in this window, often 30-50% higher than Q1 baseline for the same terms.</li>
<li>Use ad scheduling and dayparting to increase bids during forecasted heat spikes, since demand (and willingness to pay for speed) climbs sharply during multi-day heatwaves.</li>
<li>This is also the quarter with the highest available budget efficiency for Meta Ads promoting same-day availability messaging — homeowners scrolling during a heatwave respond well to "we can be there today" creative.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Q4: Furnace Season and Heating Installs</h2>
<p>October through December mirrors Q2 but for heating: pre-season inspections, early furnace replacement deals, and building the maintenance agreement base back up before winter demand peaks. Holiday-adjacent messaging ("don't spend the holidays without heat") performs well in late Q4 as urgency builds heading into the coldest months.</p>

<h2>Google Ads Seasonal Bid Adjustments and Email Drip Campaigns</h2>
<p>Beyond quarterly campaign shifts, build systematic bid adjustment rules into your Google Ads account: increase bids on repair-intent keywords 48-72 hours ahead of forecasted temperature extremes using weather-triggered scripts, and pull back on install-intent keywords during the two peak emergency months when installation crews are fully booked with repair work anyway.</p>
<p>Run a year-round email drip segmented by service history — maintenance plan members get seasonal check-in reminders, past repair customers get replacement-consideration content once their unit crosses 10-12 years old, and dormant contacts get periodic re-engagement offers. This kind of always-on nurture, layered under the seasonal paid campaigns, is what turns a single repair customer into a decade of recurring revenue. <strong>Ureb Arif</strong>, who has built seasonal campaign calendars for home services clients in similarly cyclical categories, emphasizes that the biggest single mistake HVAC companies make is treating every month like peak season — the budget discipline to pull back in Q1 is what funds the aggressive Q3 spend that actually drives annual revenue.</p>`
